N={
"batchelderschwab25_interspeech":("A community can communicate by whistling, so a listener must know how spoken vowel and stress distinctions survive when the sound source changes.","Whistling removes many speech cues and concentrates information in pitch and intensity, so a spoken-language assumption may misidentify what remains contrastive.","Assume every spoken vowel and stress cue must have the same acoustic realization in the whistle register.","Compare matched minimal pairs in spoken and whistled Greek and identify which vowel and stress contrasts are carried by F0, intensity, or neither.","Participants produce five Greek vowel qualities in Sfyria whistling and spoken Greek; acoustic contrasts are compared for stressed and unstressed forms.","The representation changes with the communication channel: F0 and intensity are alternative carriers of phonological contrast, and a missing cue is itself evidence.","All five vowel qualities remain distinct in the whistled register, but a whistled stress correlate is not found for /i/, possibly because front vowels are already highly intense.","The Sfyria community, participants, register, minimal pairs, and acoustic cues bound the result; a ceiling interpretation and cross-language generalization remain open."),
"bijoy25_interspeech":("One emotion recognizer should serve several languages without letting the largest language erase smaller languages or their emotion patterns.","Languages differ in acoustic and linguistic cues, and one teacher model may be strong in one language but transfer the wrong priorities to another.","Train one multilingual model directly or average teacher predictions without identifying which language produced the speech.","Use separate monolingual teachers and a language-aware distillation process to teach one student while preserving language-specific evidence.","Wav2vec2 teachers for English, Finnish, and French are distilled into one multilingual student and evaluated by emotion recall per language and class.","The student is a shared model with language-conditioned supervision; weighted and unweighted recall expose both overall performance and class imbalance.","The student reports weighted recall 72.9 on English and unweighted recall 63.4 on Finnish, with stronger gains for sad and neutral than anger and happiness.","Languages, emotion labels, teacher quality, class balance, and recall metrics bound the claim; multilingual transfer does not prove equal performance or culturally valid emotion categories."),
"birkholz25b_interspeech":("A physical vocal-tract replica should reveal the resonances of a modeled vowel, but the printed walls can vibrate and create false peaks and gaps.","The measurement apparatus can confuse sound transmitted through the model body with sound traveling through the intended tract cavity.","Trust the measured transfer function or change only the acoustic excitation while ignoring the replica's structural vibration.","Dampen or mechanically constrain the replica and test whether the expected resonances become cleaner and more repeatable.","Ten axisymmetric 3D-printed vowel tubes are measured with reciprocity; sound-absorbing fabric and sand embedding are compared as artifact-reduction methods.","The transfer function is a physical measurement shaped by both air paths and solid-body vibration; artifact reduction is tested through resonance structure and repeatability.","Both coverings reduce spurious poles and zeros and improve repeatability of the measured transfer functions.","Printed geometries, ten vowels, materials, reciprocity setup, and repeatability metric bound the claim; improved measurement does not prove the replica matches a human tract."),
"buech25_interspeech":("Two different tongue and lip gestures may produce similar formant changes, so a listener or analyst cannot infer articulation from one acoustic cue alone.","Labialization and pharyngealization modify different parts of the tract but can both lower F2, making a shared acoustic effect look like a shared gesture.","Treat a low F2 as proof of one particular secondary articulation.","Compare adjacent-vowel formants across both articulations, vowel qualities, and speakers, then identify which formants preserve their difference.","Thirty-five Tashlhiyt speakers produce VCV logatomes with /i, a, u/ and labialized or pharyngealized consonants; F1 and F2 patterns are compared.","The mapping is many-to-one: F2 can reveal a broad acoustic effect while F1 and vowel context retain articulatory distinctions.","Both articulations show similar F2 effects, strongest for /i/ and then /a/, while differences depend on F1 and vowel quality.","The language, speakers, logatomes, adjacent vowels, and formant measures bound the result; formants alone cannot identify every articulatory gesture."),
"carvalho25_interspeech":("A recognizer must process long recordings without making attention cost grow too quickly or forgetting what happened earlier.","Short-form transformer success does not guarantee tractable long-form decoding, and a model with local efficiency may lose useful distant context.","Apply ordinary quadratic self-attention to the entire recording or split it into chunks and discard cross-chunk memory.","Compare linear-time sequence architectures and add a non-trained nearest-neighbor memory that retrieves useful earlier representations during inference.","Fastformer, SummaryMixing, BiMamba, and E-Branchformer variants are evaluated on a new LibriHeavy long-form benchmark; KNN-MAN is added to encoder-decoder models.","Long-form recognition is a time-scale and memory problem: architecture controls cost while retrieval supplies selected history; WER across duration scales tests the tradeoff.","The paper reports a reduction from 18.8% to 17.5% WER on its LibriSpeech long-form test-clean example with BiMamba and KNN-MAN.","Benchmark construction, duration distribution, memory retrieval, architectures, and WER bound the result; a single long-form corpus does not establish general conversation robustness."),
"casanova25_interspeech":("A speech language model should generate high-quality audio quickly, but autoregressive generation must produce many codec tokens for each second of speech.","A high-rate codec can preserve detail while making training and inference slow; lowering the rate can destroy quality or causal timing.","Use a high-frame-rate codec and accept the step count, or reduce frames without testing which bitrate and causality choices caused the quality loss.","Measure the separate effects of frame rate, bitrate, and causality, then design a codec with a low frame rate that still reconstructs speech well.","NanoCodec is evaluated through codec ablations and compared across bitrate ranges, targeting 12.5 frames per second for speech LLM training and inference.","The codec converts a continuous waveform into fewer discrete generation steps; reconstruction quality and rate expose the speed-quality boundary.","The paper reports high-quality compression at 12.5 FPS and competitive performance across tested bitrates.","Codec architecture, audio data, bitrate, causality, quality metric, and hardware bound the result; low frame rate alone does not prove low end-to-end latency."),
"chen25m_interspeech":("A person with dysarthria may know the intended words but produce speech that listeners cannot understand; reconstruction should improve clarity without replacing the person's voice.","Content is hard to recover from atypical acoustics, while a system that focuses only on clarity can erase speaker identity.","Train a generic speech enhancer or normalize the voice toward an average speaker.","Separate content recovery from identity preservation and generate a clearer signal in a latent space conditioned on both.","DiffDSR uses a self-supervised speech content encoder, an in-context speaker identity encoder, and a latent diffusion generator on UASpeech.","The system has two constraints—recover phoneme content and retain speaker identity—so intelligibility and speaker similarity must be evaluated separately.","The paper reports improved speech intelligibility and speaker similarity over prior dysarthric speech reconstruction methods on UASpeech.","UASpeech, speaker identities, dysarthria types, reconstruction target, and metrics bound the claim; improved similarity and intelligibility are not clinical validation or authorship proof."),
"chi25_interspeech":("A large self-supervised speech model may be useful but too expensive to store or run, so a smaller student must retain the teacher's reusable speech knowledge.","Layer-by-layer imitation adds alignment machinery and can force the student to copy the teacher's structure rather than its learning objective.","Compress each teacher layer with a separate matching module or simply shrink the network and accept lost representations.","Replace the HuBERT teacher with a smaller student trained directly under HuBERT's iterative self-supervised objective.","DiceHuBERT distills HuBERT using the same self-supervised objective and evaluates the compact model on phoneme recognition, ASR, and SUPERB tasks.","The student learns the task's predictive structure instead of matching every hidden layer; downstream performance measures retained usefulness across tasks.","The paper reports over 21% improvement in phoneme recognition and over 14% in ASR relative to existing distillation methods, with competitive multi-task results.","Teacher/student sizes, SUPERB tasks, training data, and comparison baselines bound the result; benchmark transfer does not establish equal behavior under every deployment constraint."),
}
